Relevant columns for solving the business question  
(only those needed from NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM.NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM.NOAA_GFS0P25)

• "creation_time": limits the forecasts to those ISSUED in July‑2019 (e.g. BETWEEN 1561939200000000 AND 1564617600000000).  
• "forecast"      : VARIANT array that stores one JSON object per lead‑time (keys such as "hours", "time", "temperature_2m_above_ground", "total_precipitation_surface", "snowfall_surface", "cloud_cover_low_cloud_layer", etc.).  
• "geography"     : GEOGRAPHY point of the model grid‑node; convert with TO_GEOGRAPHY("geography") and measure distance from the target point.  
• "geography_polygon": optional – polygon of the grid‑cell; can be used with ST_WITHIN / ST_INTERSECTS if a polygon filter is preferred.  

Typical conditions you will see in queries below
------------------------------------------------
1. “creation_time” BETWEEN 1561939200000000 /*2019‑07‑01*/  
   AND             1564617599000000 /*2019‑07‑31 23:59:59*/  
2. ST_DISTANCE( TO_GEOGRAPHY('POINT(51.5 26.75)'),  
                TO_GEOGRAPHY("geography") ) <= 5000      /* 5‑km radius */  
3. f.value:"hours"::NUMBER = 24                          /* next‑day forecast */  

Ten exploratory SQL statements (from very simple to the final outline)  
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

```sql
-- 1. Description: Quick look at distinct forecast “creation_time” values in July‑2019.
SELECT DISTINCT "creation_time"
FROM NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM.NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM.NOAA_GFS0P25
WHERE "creation_time" BETWEEN 1561939200000000 AND 1564617599000000
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
