External knowledge that might be helpful: 
Categories: Geospatial functions


## ST_WITHIN

Returns true if the first geospatial object is fully contained by the second geospatial object. In other words:

The first GEOGRAPHY object g1 is fully contained by the second GEOGRAPHY object g2.
The first GEOMETRY object g1 is fully contained by the second GEOMETRY object g2.

Calling ST_WITHIN(g1, g2) is equivalent to calling ST_CONTAINS(g2, g1).
Although ST_COVEREDBY and ST_WITHIN might seem similar, the two functions have subtle differences. For details on the differences between “covered by” and “within”, see the Dimensionally Extended 9-Intersection Model (DE-9IM).

Note This function does not support using a GeometryCollection or FeatureCollection as input values.

Tip You can use the search optimization service to improve the performance of queries that call this function.
For details, see Search Optimization Service.

See also:ST_CONTAINS , ST_COVEREDBY


## Syntax

ST_WITHIN( <geography_expression_1> , <geography_expression_2> )

ST_WITHIN( <geometry_expression_1> , <geometry_expression_2> )


## Arguments


geography_expression_1A GEOGRAPHY object that is not a GeometryCollection or FeatureCollection.

geography_expression_2A GEOGRAPHY object that is not a GeometryCollection or FeatureCollection.

geometry_expression_1A GEOMETRY object that is not a GeometryCollection or FeatureCollection.

geometry_expression_2A GEOMETRY object that is not a GeometryCollection or FeatureCollection.


## Returns

BOOLEAN.

## Examples


## GEOGRAPHY examples

This shows a simple use of the ST_WITHIN function:

create table geospatial_table_01 (g1 GEOGRAPHY, g2 GEOGRAPHY);
insert into geospatial_table_01 (g1, g2) values 
    ('POLYGON((0 0, 3 0, 3 3, 0 3, 0 0))', 'POLYGON((1 1, 2 1, 2 2, 1 2, 1 1))');

Copy SELECT ST_WITHIN(g1, g2) 
    FROM geospatial_table_01;
+-------------------+
| ST_WITHIN(G1, G2) |
|-------------------|
| False             |
+-------------------+
The table structure information is ({database name: {schema name: [table name]}}): 
{'NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM': {'GEO_OPENSTREETMAP': ['HISTORY_CHANGESETS', 'PLANET_FEATURES', 'PLANET_NODES', 'PLANET_FEATURES_POINTS', 'HISTORY_NODES', 'PLANET_WAYS', 'HISTORY_LAYERS', 'HISTORY_RELATIONS', 'PLANET_RELATIONS', 'PLANET_LAYERS'], 'NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM': ['NOAA_GFS0P25']}}
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns for solving the business question  
(only those needed from NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM.NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM.NOAA_GFS0P25)

• "creation_time": limits the forecasts to those ISSUED in July‑2019 (e.g. BETWEEN 1561939200000000 AND 1564617600000000).  
• "forecast"      : VARIANT array that stores one JSON object per lead‑time (keys such as "hours", "time", "temperature_2m_above_ground", "total_precipitation_surface", "snowfall_surface", "cloud_cover_low_cloud_layer", etc.).  
• "geography"     : GEOGRAPHY point of the model grid‑node; convert with TO_GEOGRAPHY("geography") and measure distance from the target point.  
• "geography_polygon": optional – polygon of the grid‑cell; can be used with ST_WITHIN / ST_INTERSECTS if a polygon filter is preferred.  

Typical conditions you will see in queries below
------------------------------------------------
1. “creation_time” BETWEEN 1561939200000000 /*2019‑07‑01*/  
   AND             1564617599000000 /*2019‑07‑31 23:59:59*/  
2. ST_DISTANCE( TO_GEOGRAPHY('POINT(51.5 26.75)'),  
                TO_GEOGRAPHY("geography") ) <= 5000      /* 5‑km radius */  
3. f.value:"hours"::NUMBER = 24                          /* next‑day forecast */  

Ten exploratory SQL statements (from very simple to the final outline)  
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

```sql
-- 1. Description: Quick look at distinct forecast “creation_time” values in July‑2019.
SELECT DISTINCT "creation_time"
FROM NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM.NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM.NOAA_GFS0P25
WHERE "creation_time" BETWEEN 1561939200000000 AND 1564617599000000
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Display one raw JSON element of the "forecast" array for a random July‑2019 run.
SELECT f.value
FROM NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM.NOAA_GLOBAL_FORECAST_SYSTEM.NOAA_GFS0P25 t,
     LATERAL FLATTEN( input => t."forecast" ) f
WHERE t."creation_time" BETWEEN 1561939200000000 AND 1564617599000000
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Inspect key fields (hours & time) inside the flattened forecast objects.
SELECT t."creation_time",
       f.value:"hours"::NUMBER          AS "lead_hours",
       f.value:"time"                   AS "valid_time_microsec"
